{0 Code lens (reference counts)}

Above each heading something links to, a lens reading [3 references];
above the note, one reading [12 backlinks].

This is how every other language server renders this count.  VS Code writes
[3 references] over a TypeScript symbol, rust-analyzer and the Java server
do the same — a lens on its own line, with the noun spelled out.  A bare
[3] squeezed after a heading has to be decoded first.

These counts were inlay hints before ([3 refs] after the heading text).
That rendering is gone: it is this one, or nothing.  Inlay hints are now
only {!page-"feature-inlay-hints-link-direction"}.

{1 Activation}

The server advertises [codeLensProvider] already, for
{!page-"feature-command-block"}.  Counts are added to that response:

{t
  | Key                              | Type      | Default                        |
  |----------------------------------|-----------|--------------------------------|
  | [codeLens.references]            | [boolean] | [true]                         |
  | [codeLens.showReferencesCommand] | [string]  | ["editor.action.showReferences"] |
}

Clients gate code lens on their own account, and typically off: VS Code
ships [typescript.referencesCodeLens.enabled] as [false], and Zed's
[code_lens] setting is off until switched to ["on"].  A lens costs a
whole line of vertical space, which is why nobody makes the user pay it
by default.

{1 Placement}

{t
  | Count            | Lens sits above       | Title          |
  |------------------|-----------------------|----------------|
  | whole note       | line 0                | [12 backlinks] |
  | one heading      | that heading's line   | [3 references] |
}

The range is empty ([line, 0] to [line, 0]): a lens is drawn above its
range's line, and nothing about it depends on the range's width.

A count of zero produces no lens.  Most headings in a vault are pointed
at by nothing, and a column of [0 references] would be the loudest thing
on the page.

{1:self In-note references}

A note that links to its own headings is not pointed at by anything, and
a count that mixes those in with the links from elsewhere reads as more
attention than the note actually has.  The two are counted apart.

{2 Generated links do not count at all}

A link inside a [::: toc] region (see {!page-"feature-toc"}) is one the
server wrote.  It is not counted — not in the number, and not in the
locations the lens hands over on a click, so the two still agree.

This is the case that forces the rule.  A TOC names {e every} heading in
the note, so counting its links would put [1 reference] above every
heading of every note that has one — the column of numbers the
zero-suppression rule above exists to prevent, restored in full by a
single code action.  It would also make the note's own count grow by one
per heading, which is a number about the note's shape rather than about
anything pointing at it.

Find references and rename see these links as usual; only the counts
skip them.  See {!page-"feature-find-references".generated}.

{2 Authored in-note links are counted apart}

What is left after the TOC is excluded is a link the writer typed —
a map of contents by hand, a "see {i Method} below".  Those are real and
worth reporting, just not as backlinks:

{t
  | Elsewhere | In note | Note                              | Heading                                 |
  |-----------|---------|-----------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|
  | n         | 0       | [{n} backlinks]                   | [{n} references]                        |
  | n         | m       | [{n} backlinks, {m} in-note links]| [{n} references, {m} in-note references]|
  | 0         | m       | [{m} in-note links]               | [{m} in-note references]                |
}

The two numbers are {b disjoint}: [3 backlinks, 1 in-note link] is four
links, not three of which one is in-note.  That is why each half names
what it counts in full rather than trailing a bare qualifier — two named
things add up, where [3 backlinks, 1 in note] reads just as easily as a
total and a part of it.  Naming both halves also lets either appear
alone, so the third row needs no [0] to lead it.

A lens appears whenever either count is non-zero: an in-note link is
something the writer put there deliberately, and hiding it would be a
second rule to remember.

{1:wording Wording}

{t
  | Count | Note                | Heading                  |
  |-------|---------------------|--------------------------|
  | 1     | [1 backlink]        | [1 reference]            |
  | n     | [{n} backlinks]     | [{n} references]         |
  | 1 in note | [1 in-note link] | [1 in-note reference]   |
  | n in note | [{n} in-note links] | [{n} in-note references] |
}

"References" for a heading is what the editors this borrows from say.
"Backlinks" for the note is the word for the same thing in a vault, and
the two being different is useful: it says which of the two counts you
are looking at without reading the line under it.

Whichever noun a lens takes, it keeps across both halves: a heading reads
[3 references, 1 in-note reference], not two different words for the
thing it counts, and it never trades the word for another because a
number went to zero.

The note's in-note half is the exception, and only because the word it
would otherwise take contradicts itself: a backlink is by definition from
somewhere else, so there is no such thing as an in-note one.  It says
{e link}, the plain word underneath both.

"In-note {e link}", not "internal link": Obsidian calls every wikilink an
internal link — meaning inside the vault rather than out on the web — so
that phrase says the opposite of what is meant here.  "In-note" names
where the link was written, which is the distinction being drawn.

Every count is [{n} {noun}], singular and plural spelled out, the two
joined by a comma.

{1:click Clicking}

Clicking the lens opens the references it counted.  The lens carries:

{t
  | Field       | Value                                        |
  |-------------|----------------------------------------------|
  | [title]     | the count                                    |
  | [command]   | [codeLens.showReferencesCommand]             |
  | [arguments] | [\[uri; position; locations\]]                |
}

[locations] are the references themselves, from the same scan that
produced the number — so the list a click opens can never disagree with
the count above it.

{2 Why the command name is a setting}

There is no LSP request for "show me these locations".  There is a
convention: VS Code defines [editor.action.showReferences] taking
[(uri, position, locations)], and other editors implement the same names
— Zed matches [editor.action.showReferences],
[editor.action.goToLocations] and [editor.action.peekLocations], reading
the locations out of the third argument.  A convention is exactly the
kind of thing that belongs in configuration rather than in a constant:
the server says {e what} it wants shown, the client's name for it is the
user's to state.

An unrecognised name costs nothing: the client either ignores it (the
lens is then a label, which is what it was before this was wired) or
forwards it as [workspace/executeCommand], which this server answers
[None] to, since the command is not one of its own.  Setting the key to
[""] asks for the label deliberately.

A [Command] is required either way — an LSP lens shows its title through
one, so there is no such thing as a lens with a title and no command.

The keyboard route is unaffected and needs none of this: find references
on the heading.  See {!page-"feature-find-references"}.

{1 Edge cases}

{ul
  {- {b Command blocks}: a note with a command panel gets both kinds of
     lens.  The command lenses come first in the response — they are the
     note's controls, and a count should not push them down.}
  {- {b Frontmatter}: the whole-note lens sits above line 0, which is the
     opening [---] when there is frontmatter.  A lens is drawn above its
     line, so it lands at the very top of the note — which is where the
     count of the whole note belongs, and is the one thing the inlay-hint
     rendering could never get right.}
  {- {b Unsaved edits}: counts come from the indexed vault and the lines
     from the buffer, as everywhere else in the disk group.  See
     {!page-"feature-document-sync"}.}
}
